## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specifications from GNU C source code, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.

**Architecture:** A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format` (shared formatting), `magefiles/` (build orchestration), and `docs/` (specifications).

**Current state:** 17 utilities implemented through generation run 17 (tee, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum). All releases from 00.0 through 05.4 show status "implemented" in the code_status. Specifications exist for all of these plus ls (rel04.0/04.1) and ts (rel99.2).

**Current release (99.2):** The only non-implemented release with spec_complete status is rel99.2 (ts). ts is a moreutils utility that prepends timestamps to stdin lines. It has a PRD, use case, and test suite but no generated code on the working branch.

## Priority Analysis

Release 99.2 contains a single use case: `rel99.2-uc001-ts`. Per P2, implementation and tests must be split. With the 1-task constraint, I propose the implementation task first (P8: implementation before tests).

ts depends on `pkg/sys` (SIGPIPE handling) and `pkg/testutils` (for tests), both already implemented. No blocking dependencies.

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts implementation -- rel99.2-uc001-ts"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml (all requirements for ts utility)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (end-to-end execution path)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test cases and expected behaviors)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)
      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage pattern)
      - cmd/nl/main.go (reference for stdin line-processing utility pattern)
      - cmd/tac/main.go (reference for stdin-to-stdout utility with flag parsing)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: ts utility implementation -- timestamps stdin lines

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Read lines from stdin and write each line to stdout with a timestamp
          prepended. Default format is the strftime-compatible format from the PRD
          (typically "%b %d %H:%M:%S"). When no format argument is given, use the
          default. When a format argument is given, use it as the strftime format
          string. Implements prd004-ts core timestamping requirements.
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for relative (elapsed) timestamps. When -s is set,
          timestamps show elapsed time since the program started rather than wall
          clock time. The elapsed format uses the format argument if provided, or
          a default elapsed format from the PRD. Implements prd004-ts relative
          timestamp requirements.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement the -i flag for incremental timestamps. When -i is set,
          timestamps show the time elapsed since the previous line rather than
          wall clock or program-start time. -i and -s may be combined (incremental
          relative). Implements prd004-ts incremental timestamp requirements.
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Handle --version and --help flags per GNU conventions. Exit 0 on
          --version and --help. Exit 0 on normal completion. Exit nonzero on
          errors (e.g., write failures). Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at
          program start per the ARCHITECTURE shared_protocols. Implements prd004-ts
          exit code and signal requirements.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the
          ARCHITECTURE shared_protocols for all cmd/ utilities that write to stdout.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Use Go time package for timestamp formatting. Go uses reference-time
          formatting (Mon Jan 2 15:04:05 MST 2006), not strftime. Convert the
          strftime format string from the user to Go reference-time layout, or
          implement a strftime-to-Go converter. Check the PRD for the exact
          conversion requirements and which strftime directives must be supported.
      - id: D3
        text: |
          Use bufio.Scanner for line-by-line stdin reading, consistent with other
          cmd/ utilities (nl, tac, fold). Flush each line to stdout immediately
          after timestamping to avoid buffering delays in pipeline usage.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          The reference binary for differential testing is "gts" or "ts" from
          Homebrew moreutils. The stitch agent implements only cmd/ts/main.go;
          tests are a separate task.

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles with no errors via "go build ./cmd/ts/"
      - id: AC2
        text: |
          Running "echo hello | go run ./cmd/ts/" prepends a timestamp to "hello"
          and writes the result to stdout.
      - id: AC3
        text: |
          The -s flag produces elapsed timestamps relative to program start instead
          of wall clock timestamps.
      - id: AC4
        text: |
          The -i flag produces incremental timestamps showing time since the
          previous line.
      - id: AC5
        text: |
          The program calls sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() and exits cleanly when
          downstream closes the pipe.
      - id: AC6
        text: "go vet ./cmd/ts/ reports no issues."
```